// The compiled program a match runs.
//
// **The flat arrays are gone, and the reversal is the interesting part.** `classLo`/`classHi` held
// every character class's ranges end to end, with `classStart`/`classCount` indexing in, and the
// original explains itself as *"wac has no generics-free way to hold a list of structs without
// writing the container again"* — which reads like a language gap and is not one, since `Vec<Range>`
// compiles today.
//
// This file then kept the flat form anyway, on the grounds that `Vec<Range>` would cost *"a boxed
// struct per range in a matcher's inner loop"*, and called that *the third time the right answer was
// to leave something alone*. **`../../../bench/dispatchcost.wac` says the opposite**, and says it
// with a number: on this host a boxed record is **12% faster to read** than three flat lanes, because
// wasm bounds-checks every array index and a struct field is an unchecked offset. The flat form pays
// per operand; the boxed form pays per element.
//
// The build side goes the other way — 4.05 ns an element flat against 6.4 boxed — so there is a
// crossover at **about fifteen reads per element**. A class table is built once per compile and every
// range in it is scanned on every `Class` instruction of every match, which is not near fifteen.
//
// > **So the argument was right in form and wrong in sign**, and the entry it produced —
// > *a comment that reads as a workaround is worth checking before it is treated as one* — is still
// > true and was applied to the wrong conclusion. Checking it meant running it, and this directory
// > had spent five days not being able to.
//
// ## And then this file boxed the instructions, which is the same trade and the busier loop
//
// *2026-09-05.* The paragraph above refuses `Vec<Range>` because it would cost *"a boxed struct per
// range in a matcher's inner loop"*. `Op[] code` below is a boxed struct **per instruction**, and the
// matcher touches an instruction on every step and a range only inside a `Class`.
//
// The size is worse than it looks. `../../../TECHNICAL.md`'s lowering and
// `@/packages/wacc/src/emit.wac` agree that an enum is **one wasm struct — a tag, then a slot per
// payload field of every variant**. Counting `Op`'s: `byte`, `index`, `target`, `first`, `second`,
// `slot`, `kind`, `loop`, `loop`, `slot` — **ten payload fields plus the tag, so eleven slots per
// instruction**, against the original's three `i32` in a flat array. And `WordBoundary(Boundary)`
// makes that slot a reference to a second struct, so one instruction is two heap objects.
//
// **`@/packages/wacc/src/lex.wac` reached the opposite conclusion about the identical trade**, and
// argued it out:
//
// > `Token[]` is therefore a **regression**, and the flat `i32[]` is correct. What the struct buys —
// > five named fields instead of `tokens[i * 5 + 2]` — has to be bought some other way.
//
// Two files, one directory, one week, one question — *does an array of records become an array of
// references?* — and the answer is yes in both. `lex.wac` accepted it and this file did not notice
// it had made the same choice while arguing against it two paragraphs up.
//
// **The difference that might rescue it, and it is not the one I would have guessed.** A token stream
// is built per compile and thrown away; a `Program` is built once and matched many times, so its
// eleven slots are amortised over every subsequent match while a token's five are not. That is an
// argument about *how often the array is built*, and the paragraph above is an argument about *how
// often it is read* — and those point opposite ways here. Neither file made the distinction, and
// nothing in this directory can measure which dominates.
//
// ## Measured, and it went the other way
//
// `../../../bench/dispatchcost.wac`, v8, 67.1M dispatch steps per arm, best of two:
//
//       prog     steps     flat   narrow     wide    again
//         16  67108864       82       68       68       82
//        256  67108864       78       69       69       78
//       4096  67108864       77       68       68       76
//
// **The boxed forms are 12% faster**, and eleven fields cost the same as three. Wasm bounds-checks
// every array index: `code[pc * 3 + k]` is three checked loads and `code[pc]` plus two struct fields
// is one. The flat form pays per *operand*; the boxed form pays per *instruction*.
//
// So `Op[]` is right, for a reason this file did not give — and **the paragraph at the top of this
// header is the one now in doubt**, because *a boxed struct per range in a matcher's inner loop* is
// exactly the cost the bench says is negative on the read side. What would rescue it is the build
// side, which the bench deliberately excludes: a class table is built per compile like a token
// stream, not once per program like the code array. Nobody has measured that.

import { Bytes, Vec } from "core";

/**
 * One instruction.
 *
 * Thirteen `export i32 OP_CHAR() { return 0; }` in the original — functions returning literals,
 * numbered by hand, in a file whose other integers are program counters and capture slots. A
 * `match` over an enum is checked and a `switch` over an `i32` is not.
 */
export enum Op {
  Char(u8 byte),
  Any,                          // any byte except newline
  Class(i32 index),             // into the class table
  Match,
  Jmp(i32 target),
  Split(i32 first, i32 second), // try `first`, and on failure `second`
  Save(i32 slot),               // capture slot
  Bol,
  Eol,
  WordBoundary(Boundary kind),
  Mark(i32 loop),               // remember where this iteration began
  Progress(i32 loop),           // fail if the iteration consumed nothing
  Clear(i32 slot)
}

/**
 * `\b`, `\B`, `\<`, `\>`.
 *
 * These were `a: 1 \b, 0 \B, 2 \< (word start), 3 \> (word end)` — four meanings in one operand
 * field, documented in a trailing comment, with `0` for the negated case so a zeroed operand reads
 * as `\B` rather than as nothing.
 */
export enum Boundary { At, NotAt, WordStart, WordEnd }

/** One `[a-z0-9]`. `negated` is `[^…]`. */
export struct CharClass {
  Vec<Range> ranges;
  bool negated;
}

/** An inclusive byte range. Two fields, read on every `Class` step — the boxed side of the crossover. */
export struct Range { i32 lo; i32 hi; }

export struct Program {
  /** **Eleven slots and a heap object per instruction** — see the header. Kept, and now argued. */
  Op[] code;
  i32 groups;           // capturing groups, so slots = (groups + 1) * 2
  i32 loops;            // loop slots needed by Mark/Progress

  /** The character classes `Class(i)` indexes. Five parallel arrays until the bench said otherwise. */
  Vec<CharClass> classes;

  /** Capture slots: two per group, plus two for the whole match. */
  i32 slotCount(const this) { return (this.groups + 1) * 2; }
}
